 Hi! This question involves a series of fractions. Each fraction in the series has a numerator of 1 and a denominator that is the product of two consecutive integers. For example, the first fraction is $$\frac{1}{1\cdot 2} = \frac{1}{2}.$$ The denominator is the product of the term's position in the series (n) and the next term (n+1). For the nth term, the denominator is n(n+1). 
标语 <end think></end think>
<answer> Notice that each term can be rewritten as the difference of two fractions:
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{n(n+1)} &= \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1} \\
&= \frac{n+1}{n(n+1)} - \frac{n}{n(n+1)} \\
&= \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1}
\end{align}
Thus, every term in the series is the difference of two consecutive fractions, except for the first and last terms, which do not have such a pattern. By writing out the first few terms and factoring out most of the terms, we can see that this series telescopes:
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{1\cdot 2} + \frac{1}{2\cdot 3} + \frac{1}{3\cdot 4} + \frac{1}{4\cdot 5} + \dots + \frac{1}{9\cdot 10}\\
&= \left(\frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{3}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{4}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{5}\right) + \dots + \left(\frac{1}{9} - \frac{1}{10}\right)\\
&= \frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{10}\\
&= \frac{10}{10} - \frac{1}{10}\\
&= \frac{9}{10}
\end{align*}
Therefore, the sum of the series is \(\boxed{\frac{9}{10}}\).
